window.docContents[66].resolve({"/rules/PL1004/#PL1004-configuration":{"contents":"No source option, attribute or command-line flag makes this rule pass on a claimed surface. set_option linter.plumb false and plumb.localFoundation never waive it: lake lint, the build-lint policy target and axiomGate still apply the rule. Where Plumb's local linter reports a finding during a project build (axiomGate and the policy target keep it on by default; lake lint builds with it off, whatever the source sets linter.plumb to), that finding is a build warning, so the result is INCOMPLETE under PL2003 instead of carrying this rule's finding. Switching the local linter off changes which finding is reported, never whether the result is accepted. Hiding the diagnostic does not establish the property it checks.\n\nAuthenticated native proofs are reported separately in documentation teaching mode (lean-trusted-compiler fences) and remain excluded from conforming positives.\n\nProject enforcement runs through lake lint (incremental), lake lint -- --fresh and lake exe axiomGate (fresh whole-project audits from empty build output), and the build-lint policy target. See the adoption guide.\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL1004: Compiler-trusting proofs require separate classification","header":"Configuration and exceptions","id":"/rules/PL1004/#PL1004-configuration"},"/rules/PL2003/#PL2003-configuration":{"contents":"warningAsError := false in the source cannot hide a warning from the audit. Disabling a linter (for example set_option linter.unusedVariables false) can stop it from emitting, which makes this rule pass without discharging the property the linter checks; do not do it.\n\nProject enforcement runs through lake lint (incremental), lake lint -- --fresh and lake exe axiomGate (fresh whole-project audits from empty build output), and the build-lint policy target. See the adoption guide.\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL2003: Claimed source must elaborate warning-free","header":"Configuration and exceptions","id":"/rules/PL2003/#PL2003-configuration"},"/rules/PL3002/#PL3002-configuration":{"contents":"execution in the surface manifest (report or checked), or --execution checked for a single-file audit, selects the mode. report mode reports trusted boundaries without failing them; it is not a fix for a checked claim.\n\nProject enforcement runs through lake lint (incremental), lake lint -- --fresh and lake exe axiomGate (fresh whole-project audits from empty build output), and the build-lint policy target. See the adoption guide.\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL3002: Checked execution requires admitted correspondence","header":"Configuration and exceptions","id":"/rules/PL3002/#PL3002-configuration"},"/rules/PL4001/#PL4001-configuration":{"contents":"The checked tree is the documentation tree the audit selects (docs/ for lake exe docFenceAudit). There is no per-fence opt-out.\n\nThis rule is a documentation-mode rule; it is not reported by the per-declaration editor linter.\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009PL4001: Documentation fences must have a valid classification","header":"Configuration and exceptions","id":"/rules/PL4001/#PL4001-configuration"},"/versions/#versions-routes":{"contents":"* https://rbeauchamp.github.io/lean-plumb/dev/rules/<ID>/ is the development explanation. It always shows the most recent successfully deployed revision of main; the linter's diagnostics link here.* https://rbeauchamp.github.io/lean-plumb/rev/<commit>/rules/<ID>/ is the snapshot of one published commit. Each deployment publishes the snapshot of its own commit and every earlier published snapshot, byte for byte: a snapshot is archived before it is deployed, the archive is append-only, and a deployment must contain every archived snapshot.* https://rbeauchamp.github.io/lean-plumb/v/<version>/rules/<ID>/ is reserved for immutable pages of released packages. No package has been released, so no such page exists.\n\nA route that is not published shows the site's not-available page, which names the source of every revision on GitHub. It never redirects to the latest rules: an old link cannot silently acquire changed semantics. Rule IDs are never reused for a changed rule; a retired rule keeps a page that says so.\n\n","context":"Plumb for Lean rule reference\u0009Versions and evidence","header":"Routes","id":"/versions/#versions-routes"}});